{"timestamp":"2026-07-24T10:03:51.949303","user":"Differentiate f(x) = x^3 - 4x + 7. Return the derivative and one short justification.","nova":"The derivative of \\( f(x) = x^3 - 4x + 7 \\) is \\( f'(x) = 3x^2 - 4 \\). This can be justified by applying the power rule for differentiation, which states that if you have a function like \\( ax^n \\), its derivative is \\( anx^{n-1} \\). In this case, we differentiate each term separately: the derivative of \\( x^3 \\) is \\( 3x^2 \\), and the derivative of \\( -4x \\) is \\( -4 \\), leaving us with \\( f'(x) = 3x^2 - 4 \\)."}
