{"timestamp": "2026-09-03T19:24:51.146674", "user": "Explain the tradeoffs between eventual consistency and strong consistency in a distributed system in three short paragraphs.", "nova": "In a distributed system, eventual consistency and strong consistency are two different approaches to handling data consistency across multiple nodes. Strong consistency ensures that all nodes agree on the state of the data at any given time, which guarantees that once a transaction completes, the data is consistent across all nodes. This is achieved by requiring all nodes to reach the same state simultaneously. On the other hand, eventual consistency allows for partial agreement among nodes, where nodes may not reach the same state immediately but will eventually converge. This approach provides better scalability and fault tolerance but comes with the tradeoff of potentially inconsistent data until the system is fully synchronized."}
